1
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.191
Wash your hands and introduce yourself confirming the patient's identity.

2
00:00:09.191 --> 00:00:12.049
Prepare the otoscope in front of the patient.

3
00:00:12.049 --> 00:00:19.191
Confirm the light is bright and use a large speculum for an adult and a smaller one for a child.

4
00:00:19.191 --> 00:00:23.477
While doing this explain the procedure gaining consent to examine the ears.

5
00:00:23.477 --> 00:00:29.549
With the patient seated, ask if they have ear symptoms and inspect the more normal one first.

6
00:00:29.549 --> 00:00:35.620
Pay attention to the pina and behind the mastoid are you looking for any redness and swelling.

7
00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:42.580
Note scars, pits, sinuses, fistually, broken skin or pus in and around the tragus and pre-urricular

8
00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.580
areas.

9
00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.900
Before autoscopy, wipe away any pus and gently palpate the tragus for tenderness.

10
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.020
Ask about pain and observe the patient for any wincing.

11
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.578
When examining the right ear, use your right hand holding the arc light like

12
00:00:57.578 --> 00:00:58.340
a pen, steadying

13
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.580
your hand on the cheek.

14
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:05.660
As the ear canal is curved, pull the pinna upwards and backwards to straighten it.

15
00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.460
And children pull straight back.

16
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.224
Place a speculum tip by the opening of the ear canal, then bring your

17
00:01:12.224 --> 00:01:13.300
eye in close to

18
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.140
view the external matus and the canal.

19
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:23.380
You are likely to see hairs and wax, note any foreign bodies or redness.

20
00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:27.959
Remind the patient you are going to advance the tip of the speculum further

21
00:01:27.959 --> 00:01:28.940
into the canal.

22
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.770
to speculate gently and slowly forwards and upwards in the direction of the eye.

23
00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:34.460
Do this

24
00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.559
until you can see the tympanic membrane.

25
00:01:37.559 --> 00:01:41.100
If you cannot see the membrane, reposition, remembering

26
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.956
to point towards the eye.

27
00:01:42.956 --> 00:01:47.780
If there is still no view, then the canal may be stenosed or

28
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.547
an ear washout may be needed to remove wax or pus.

29
00:01:52.547 --> 00:01:55.580
If it is too painful, then stop.

30
00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:01.380
Once you see the membrane, examine in a circular motion following the annulus, taking note

31
00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.740
of the main features.

32
00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:10.980
These can include the cone of light, umbil, pars flaccida, lateral process of malleus,

33
00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:15.780
incus, handle of malleus and pars tensor.

34
00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:20.660
Signs of disease may include scarring of

35
00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:25.540
the membrane, redness and new vessels, pus,

36
00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:33.140
sucking in of the membrane and any perforations, both small and large.

37
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:40.420
After the examination, thank the patient, explain your findings and finish by washing your hands

38
00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:48.820
and the speculum.
